      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "System overview",
      "pageStart": 2,
      "pageEnd": 2,
      "target": "../sections/sec-003-system-overview.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "We based our system on the Annif automated sub-\nject indexing toolkit. It provides a selection of\nXMTC algorithms as configurable backends which\ncan be used for subject indexing by setting up\nprojects that define the vocabulary and specific con-\nfiguration settings of a backend.\nWe chose three Annif backends for this task: 1)\nOmikuji5, an implementation of a family of effi-\ncient machine learning algorithms for multilabel\nclassification based on the idea of partitioned la-\nbel trees, including Parabel (Prabhu et al., 2018)\nand Bonsai (Khandagale et al., 2020). We used the\nBonsai-style configur",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "System overview. Three Annif backends were used: Omikuji in a Bonsai-style configuration (partitioned label trees); MLLM, a Maui-like lexical matcher of text to vocabulary terms; and XTransformer, a fine-tuned Transformer XMTC model from the PECOS framework based on xlm-roberta-base, whose Annif integration was experimental and refined during the task. Backends were combined in simple ensembles, which average their scores, and neural ensembles, which also train a network to adjust scores, for example to suppress frequent false positives.",
